+// Page tools of plan C15 for tabs that live in the embedding app's own UI (no
+// Browser window): DevTools, printing and Save Page As. Menu items: [01 §File]
+// "Save Page As", "Print"; [01 §View] Developer ▸ "Developer Tools", "Inspect
+// Elements", "JavaScript Console", "Network Inspector" (observed names).
+
+// What View ▸ Developer ▸ asks DevTools to do.
+enum class DevToolsCommand {
+  // Developer Tools: open, or bring forward, on the last panel used.
+  kShow,
+  // Inspect Elements: the Elements panel in element-picking mode, like
+  // Chrome's "Inspect elements" command.
+  kInspectElement,
+  // JavaScript Console, Network Inspector: the panel named by `panel`
+  // ("console", "network"; DevTools' own panel ids).
+  kShowPanel,
+};
+
+// Shows Chrome's DevTools front end for `inspected` in a window of its own,
+// one per inspected tab; a second command reuses it. Chrome docks DevTools
+// into its Browser window, which Open Arc does not have, and the spec records
+// nothing about docking, so the front end gets a separate resizable window
+// (inferred). The window closes with its tab, with ⌘W, or with its close
+// button. Does nothing when policy forbids DevTools for the profile.
+void ShowDevTools(content::WebContents* inspected,
+                  DevToolsCommand command,
+                  const std::string& panel);
+
+// Closes the DevTools window of `inspected`, if any.
+void CloseDevTools(content::WebContents* inspected);
+
+// File ▸ Print: the macOS print panel for the page (or the focused frame with
+// a selection). Chrome's print preview is a dialog hosted by a Browser
+// window's tab-modal host, which embedder-owned tabs do not have, so Open Arc
+// runs with print preview disabled (--disable-print-preview) and prints
+// through the system panel, which needs only the page's view. Also what
+// window.print() shows.
+void PrintPage(content::WebContents* contents);
+
+// File ▸ Save Page As formats (Chrome's three; the spec records only the menu
+// item, so the choice is inferred).
+enum class SavePageFormat {
+  kComplete,    // HTML plus a "<name>_files" folder with the resources.
+  kHtmlOnly,    // The page's HTML only.
+  kSingleFile,  // One MHTML file.
+};
+
+// Saves the page at `path`. A page that is not HTML (a PDF, an image, plain
+// media) is saved as its original file instead, whatever `format` says, as
+// Chrome does. The save runs as a download of the tab's profile, so it shows
+// in the downloads list. `done` runs once the save started (true) or could
+// not start (false); it does not wait for the file to be written.
+void SavePage(content::WebContents* contents,
+              const base::FilePath& path,
+              SavePageFormat format,
+              base::OnceCallback<void(bool started)> done);
+
